# {{PACK_NAME}}

A sample Kindgi pack — scaffolded by `kindgi init --template=sample`.

Two tools (`echo`, `greet`), one guardrail (`response-not-empty`), one
agent that uses both tools + the guardrail, and one flow that stitches
the two tools into a workflow. It is compile-time-valid and ready to
run with `kindgi dev`.

## Layout

```
{{PACK_NAME}}/
├── kindgi.config.ts                       # pack id + version + discovery patterns
├── package.json                              # depends on @kindgi/sdk + zod
├── tsconfig.json
├── tools/
│   ├── echo/index.ts                         # defineTool — Zod-authored echo
│   └── greet/index.ts                        # defineTool — Zod-authored greeting
├── guardrails/
│   └── response-not-empty/index.ts           # defineCheck — response has content
├── agents/
│   └── echo-agent/index.ts                   # defineAgent — uses both tools + guardrail
└── flows/
    └── echo-flow/index.ts                    # Flow — greet tool → echo agent step → declared output
```

## Run

```sh
pnpm install
pnpm typecheck                                # verify the pack compiles
kindgi dev                                 # runs the Kindgi runtime (Docker) + hot-reloads this pack
```

`kindgi dev` bundles every primitive under the four folders above,
runs their code in a local pack service, starts the Kindgi runtime as a
Docker container (with a local Postgres), registers this pack, and
re-registers on every file save.

## Use a real model

`kindgi dev` gives a new pack `dev-echo`, a fallback provider that isn't a
model: it calls the first tool and replies "Tool responded: …", after a
warning line that says so. It answers only while no other provider fits. To
use a real model, set one LLM provider's key and register its preset, with
`kindgi dev` running, from another terminal. For Claude:

```sh
kindgi secrets set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY --env=local --scope=tenant   # no-echo prompt; writes .env.local
kindgi providers register --preset=anthropic                      # Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Haiku 4.5
```

Claude takes over from dev-echo on the next turn; nothing to restart.
`--models=claude-haiku-4-5` registers one model only. OpenAI works the same
way (`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `--preset=openai`), and so does OpenRouter, many
vendors' models with one key (`OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, `--preset=openrouter`);
`kindgi providers presets` lists the presets (Gemini on Vertex AI too). Picking a model per
agent, and other providers: `.claude/skills/kindgi-authoring-providers/SKILL.md`.

## The four primitives (one file each)

| Kind | File | What it shows |
|---|---|---|
| Tool | `tools/echo/index.ts` | `defineTool` with Zod input/output; no `needs`; `effects: []`. |
| Tool | `tools/greet/index.ts` | Second Zod-authored tool — takes a name, returns a greeting. |
| Tool | `tools/fetch-httpbin/index.ts` | `defineTool({spec: {kind: 'http'}})` — declarative HTTP call, no imperative handler code. Hits httpbin.org so it works without credentials. |
| Guardrail | `guardrails/response-not-empty/index.ts` | `defineCheck` — zero-LLM check that a response payload is non-empty. |
| Agent | `agents/echo-agent/index.ts` | `defineAgent` — pulls in the local tools + the guardrail; uses `capabilities` for model routing. |
| Flow | `flows/echo-flow/index.ts` | `defineFlow` — a tool step fed from the run input, an agent step, a declared `output`. Run: `kindgi runs start --flow={{PACK_ID}}.echo-flow --input='{"name":"Ada"}'`. |

## Extend

Add more primitives following the same folder convention. `defineTool` +
`defineCheck` + `defineAgent` all come from `@kindgi/sdk/define`:

```ts
import { defineTool, defineCheck, defineAgent, defineFlow } from '@kindgi/sdk/define';
import { z } from 'zod';
```

Every `define*` export ships with JSDoc — hover it in your editor for
field-level docs.

## Note on `@kindgi/sdk` versioning

This template pins `@kindgi/sdk: "workspace:*"` because the SDK is not
yet published to npm. Until it is, add the pack to a pnpm workspace that
contains the Kindgi packages (`pnpm-workspace.yaml`).
